Consider the triangular grid in the plane. A benzel is a hexagonal-grid region defined as follows: for positive integers $a, b$ with $2 \le a \le 2b$, $2 \le b \le 2a$, the $(a,b)$-benzel is the region obtained from the regular hexagon $\{(x,y,z) \in \mathbb{R}^3 : x+y+z=0,\ -a \le x,y \le a,\ -b \le z \le b\}$ (projected to the plane) by removing certain corner triangles in the standard way, yielding a region tileable (in some cases) by the following prototiles in the triangular grid:
\begin{itemize}
\item a "right stone": a rhombus consisting of two adjacent unit triangles oriented in one fixed way;
\item a "left stone": the mirror-image rhombus;
\item three "bones" (tribones): straight trominoes of three unit triangles, in each of the three possible orientations.
\end{itemize}

For $i, j \in \{0,1\}$ and $k \in \{0,1,2,3\}$, let $T_{ijk}(a,b)$ denote the number of tilings of the $(a,b)$-benzel using a set of prototiles that contains the right stone iff $i=1$, contains the left stone iff $j=1$, and contains exactly $k$ of the three bone orientations (any $k$-subset, with $T_{ijk}$ summing over choices when ambiguous; here we focus on $k=3$, so all bone orientations are allowed).

To each $(a,b)$-benzel one associates the Conway–Lagarias invariant $\mathrm{CL}(a,b) \in \mathbb{Z}$, an integer-valued invariant of the region (independent of any tiling) computed from the signed area enclosed by the shadow path of its boundary. It is known that if $\mathrm{CL}(a,b) < 0$, then the $(a,b)$-benzel admits no tiling by bones and right-pointing stones, so $T_{103}(a,b) = 0$.

Question: Is it true that whenever $\mathrm{CL}(a,b) \ge 0$, one has $T_{103}(a,b) \ge 1$? Equivalently, does the $(a,b)$-benzel admit at least one tiling using right stones and any of the three bone orientations (no left stones) whenever the Conway–Lagarias invariant is nonnegative?

